---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import { getPost } from '../../posts';
import '../../styles/posts/the-rotation.css';
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const post = getPost('the-rotation');

// rotation.js is served verbatim from public/ (Astro doesn't fingerprint it),
// so append a build-time content hash as ?v= for cache-busting. The hash
// changes only when the file changes, so returning browsers fetch fresh JS on
// every edit without a manual hard-refresh, and keep the cached copy otherwise.
const rotationJs = readFileSync('public/posts/the-rotation/rotation.js');
const jsVersion = createHash('sha256').update(rotationJs).digest('hex').slice(0, 12);
---
<PostLayout post={post}>
<div class="article">

  <!-- Interactive gym checklist — built in JS from the authored program data.
       See public/posts/the-rotation/rotation.js (generated from
       fitscrape/data/strength/program.json). -->
  <div id="rotation" class="rotation-app"></div>

  <p class="rot-closer">Run a rotation until it&rsquo;s boring. <b>Then climb a notch and rotate.</b></p>

  <section class="rot-notes">
    <h3 class="rot-notes__h">Notes</h3>
    <ol class="rot-notes__list">
      <li><b>Where this comes from.</b> The program is authored from the standard strength
        literature &mdash; Rippetoe&rsquo;s <i>Practical Programming</i> and <i>Starting Strength</i>,
        Wendler&rsquo;s <i>5/3/1</i>, Helms&rsquo; <i>Muscle &amp; Strength Pyramid</i>, and
        Israetel&rsquo;s hypertrophy work. The four barbell lifts run a 5/3/1 wave; accessories run
        double progression; the A&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;B&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;C rotation swaps in new variants
        and climbs in load each pass.</li>
      <li><b>New to the barbell?</b> Don&rsquo;t start here &mdash; run a linear novice progression
        first (squat 3&times;5 adding weight every session; deadlift 1&times;5; bench/press 3&times;5).
        Graduate to the rotation once linear gains stall, usually after 8&ndash;16 weeks.</li>
      <li><b>Reading the loads.</b> Build your Training Max at 90% of a true 1RM and let the
        wave do the rest. The <b>YouTube</b> button on any exercise pulls up a form demo; the
        <b>Progress</b> line tells you how to advance that specific movement.</li>
      <li><b>Not medical advice.</b> Warm up, keep 1&ndash;2 reps in the tank on the work sets, and
        stop a set the moment form breaks.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <script src={`/posts/the-rotation/rotation.js?v=${jsVersion}`} is:inline></script>
</div>
</PostLayout>
